 \section{Procedure}
 Participants perform an agent-guided object-search task while seated on a swivel chair, with multiple colored geometric objects scattered across eight surrounding vertical planes. Although the application knows the target identity, the participant does not receive a color-and-shape description before searching. Instead, the agent guides the participant toward the target wall and then refines the direction horizontally, vertically or diagonally. The participant selects an object using the right-controller ray and trigger; looking at an object, receiving a gaze confirmation or pointing at it does not itself complete the trial.
 
-Each trial begins with the participant looking at a readiness cross. Once readiness is established, an opening directional cue plays while the objects remain hidden, and object reveal then starts active search timing. A correct trigger selection before the 15-second active-search deadline stops timing and produces success feedback; an incorrect selection is counted and search continues within that same limit. This sequence separates readiness and opening guidance from the timed search interval while retaining their timestamps for analysis.
+Each trial begins with the participant looking at a readiness cross. Once readiness is established, an opening directional cue plays while the objects remain hidden, and object reveal then starts active search timing. A correct trigger selection before the 25-second active-search deadline stops timing and produces success feedback; an incorrect selection is counted and search continues within that same limit. This sequence separates readiness and opening guidance from the timed search interval while retaining their timestamps for analysis.
 
-If the target has not been selected after 15 seconds of active search, the trial ends as a timeout. The application hides the objects, cancels pending spatial hints, and plays a prepared encouragement in the assigned voice, beginning with ``Time up,'' before presenting the next readiness cross. For example, the agent may say, ``Time up. Let's shake that round off and start the next.'' Separate wording introduces the block break or acknowledges the final trial. Timeout feedback is outside the search interval and does not imply a correct selection. The same deadline and phrase set apply to both voice conditions and to the single practice trial; a timeout advances the scheduled attempt without adding a replacement. Explicit pauses suspend the clock, whereas tracking loss alone does not. The 15-second limit is a researcher-selected protocol parameter introduced on October 23, 2026, rather than a threshold established by the cited studies.
+If the target has not been selected after 25 seconds of active search, the trial ends as a timeout. The application hides the objects, cancels pending spatial hints, and plays a prepared encouragement in the assigned voice, beginning with ``Time up,'' before presenting the next readiness cross. For example, the agent may say, ``Time up. Let's shake that round off and start the next.'' Separate wording introduces the block break or acknowledges the final trial. Timeout feedback is outside the search interval and does not imply a correct selection. The same deadline and phrase set apply to both voice conditions and to the single practice trial; a timeout advances the scheduled attempt without adding a replacement. Explicit pauses suspend the clock, whereas tracking loss alone does not. The 25-second limit is a researcher-selected protocol parameter introduced on October 23, 2026, rather than a threshold established by the cited studies.
 
 Before participant use, consent materials must be reconciled with ElevenLabs voice processing and Jev cloud decisions, including the data transmitted, retention, access, and deletion arrangements. This reconciliation remains a requirement before participant recruitment. After consent, participants complete background questions and baseline symptom ratings. They then choose a neutral voice, rate auditioned voices, record a standard passage in their natural voice for synthesis, and confirm comfortable, clear audio for each condition. The retained English recording passage is reproduced in Appendix~\ref{app:instruments}, although its match to the frozen build and typical recording duration remain to be verified. Preferred gender presentation is chosen explicitly rather than inferred from participant gender.
 
